WEBVTT

00:04:28.764 --> 00:04:30.641
yeah, and they all, by the way, did it,

00:04:30.683 --> 00:04:34.395
because philosophy professors
love talking about philosophy.

00:04:35.187 --> 00:04:36.397
The drop of a hat,

00:04:36.397 --> 00:04:38.816
they will all talk
about philosophy with you.

00:04:39.150 --> 00:04:40.318
So in the process,

00:04:40.359 --> 00:04:43.362
I learn all of these incredibly
wonderful theories

00:04:43.404 --> 00:04:46.032
that the smartest people
who have ever lived

00:04:46.073 --> 00:04:48.826
have developed over the last 2,500 years

00:04:48.868 --> 00:04:52.163
that help us make better decisions
and become better people.

00:04:52.663 --> 00:04:55.249
For example, I learned about Immanuel Kant

00:04:55.291 --> 00:04:56.834
and the categorical imperative.

00:04:56.876 --> 00:04:59.295
So Kant says, when we're
about to do something

00:04:59.295 --> 00:05:01.964
we have to design a rule or a maxim